Senator Mark A. Villar: Thank you, Mr. President.

asr-007428
05:28:25.420 --> 05:28:28.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: And thank you to the economic team.

asr-007429
05:28:28.420 --> 05:28:30.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: I hope, I just have a few,

asr-007430
05:28:30.420 --> 05:28:33.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: I start off with some macroeconomic questions

asr-007431
05:28:33.420 --> 05:28:37.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: and then we'll move on to maybe some fiscal questions I have

asr-007432
05:28:37.420 --> 05:28:39.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: and then maybe some private questions.

asr-007433
05:28:39.420 --> 05:28:41.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: So I hope your other agencies are still here

asr-007434
05:28:41.420 --> 05:28:43.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: so that they can quickly answer the questions.

asr-007435
05:28:43.420 --> 05:28:47.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: Also, thank you for saying, notice that all the secretaries are still here.

asr-007436
05:28:47.420 --> 05:28:52.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: So I hope that even your other heads of agency are also still here

asr-007437
05:28:52.420 --> 05:28:55.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: so that if we have questions, we can be faster

asr-007438
05:28:55.420 --> 05:28:56.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: and for your benefit as well.

asr-007439
05:28:56.420 --> 05:28:58.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: So thank you very much.

asr-007440
05:28:58.420 --> 05:28:59.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: My first question is,

asr-007441
05:29:00.420 --> 05:29:04.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: are with regards to the,

asr-007442
05:29:04.420 --> 05:29:06.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: well, earlier we were speaking about the growth,

asr-007443
05:29:06.420 --> 05:29:09.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: which is,

asr-007444
05:29:09.420 --> 05:29:13.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: as the last quarter, I believe it was 2.1%.

asr-007445
05:29:13.420 --> 05:29:15.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: And,

asr-007446
05:29:15.420 --> 05:29:17.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: well, I'm sure, of course,

asr-007447
05:29:17.420 --> 05:29:21.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: in terms of GDP, the breakdown is basically consumption,

asr-007448
05:29:21.420 --> 05:29:25.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: investment, government spending, and net exports.

asr-007449
05:29:25.420 --> 05:29:28.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: I would like to know first,

asr-007450
05:29:28.420 --> 05:29:30.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: what is more or less the breakdown

asr-007451
05:29:30.420 --> 05:29:32.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: of the growth rate?

asr-007452
05:29:32.420 --> 05:29:39.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: And where, if ever, did we see a large,

asr-007453
05:29:39.420 --> 05:29:43.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: what basically fueled the decrease or the low GDP?

asr-007454
05:29:43.420 --> 05:29:45.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: What sectors specifically?

asr-007455
05:29:45.420 --> 05:29:47.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: Where are the sectors that experienced low growth

asr-007456
05:29:47.420 --> 05:29:49.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: that dragged down our GDP?

asr-007457
05:29:49.420 --> 05:29:51.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: And I think it's important to understand this

asr-007458
05:29:51.420 --> 05:29:53.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: because in order for us to solve these issues,

asr-007459
05:29:53.420 --> 05:29:57.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: it's important that we understand the breakdown of our GDP growth.

asr-007460
05:29:57.420 --> 05:29:59.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: So, I mean, that's my first question.

asr-007461
05:29:59.420 --> 05:30:00.420
Senator Mark A. Villar: Thank you.

asr-007463
05:30:08.000 --> 05:30:20.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: My Honor, Mr. President, for this year, unfortunately, all sectors experienced decline, except the export sector.

asr-007464
05:30:20.000 --> 05:30:28.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: It's the only industry that has positive growth for the year.

asr-007465
05:30:28.000 --> 05:30:32.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Did you not say earlier it was zero growth? Wasn't there a statement earlier made?

asr-007466
05:30:32.000 --> 05:30:33.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: That's manufacturing.

asr-007467
05:30:33.000 --> 05:30:34.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Manufacturing.

asr-007468
05:30:34.000 --> 05:30:35.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Yes.

asr-007469
05:30:35.000 --> 05:30:37.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: So export was growing, actually grew this year.

asr-007470
05:30:37.000 --> 05:30:38.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Yes.

asr-007471
05:30:38.000 --> 05:30:50.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Where did we experience the lowest growth? What sector specifically dragged down our GDP? The largest negative.

asr-007472
05:30:50.000 --> 05:30:57.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: According to Secretary Balisaga, I'd just like to clarify that all sectors grew except that the growth was

asr-007473
05:30:58.000 --> 05:31:01.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: slower, Your Honor, Mr. President.

asr-007474
05:31:01.000 --> 05:31:03.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: All sectors grew.

asr-007475
05:31:14.000 --> 05:31:19.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: Except for agriculture also, negative.

asr-007476
05:31:19.000 --> 05:31:20.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Negative what?

asr-007477
05:31:20.000 --> 05:31:21.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: Yes, except agriculture.

asr-007478
05:31:21.000 --> 05:31:23.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: What was the negative agriculture?

asr-007479
05:31:23.000 --> 05:31:24.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: 4.3.

asr-007480
05:31:24.000 --> 05:31:26.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Negative 4.3.

asr-007481
05:31:26.000 --> 05:31:27.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: What was the growth?

asr-007482
05:31:28.000 --> 05:31:32.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: What was the cause for the negative 4? That's quite sharp.

asr-007483
05:31:32.000 --> 05:31:41.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Was there a specific climate event that caused a sharp decline? What do you attribute this low growth to?

asr-007484
05:31:41.000 --> 05:31:47.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: I think it's the monsoon rains and the flooding which affected the central zone more especially.

asr-007485
05:31:47.000 --> 05:31:52.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Was it especially strong this year as opposed to other years?

asr-007486
05:31:52.000 --> 05:31:56.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Because, I mean, we've always, and this is not, is there some new?

asr-007487
05:31:56.000 --> 05:32:01.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: I don't think the El Nino has not taken effect yet as far as I know.

asr-007488
05:32:01.000 --> 05:32:04.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: It's the flooding. It's the land, it's the land before the El Nino.

asr-007489
05:32:04.000 --> 05:32:18.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: It took a while before, according to Secretary De Leon, that it took a while for the flooding to subside in the agricultural, in the rice producing provinces, Your Honor, Mr. President.

asr-007490
05:32:18.000 --> 05:32:19.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Especially central zone.

asr-007491
05:32:19.000 --> 05:32:25.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: So are we expecting this to rebound in the next, the latter part of the year?

asr-007492
05:32:25.000 --> 05:32:29.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: Now, after the monsoon rains, it's now El Nino.

asr-007493
05:32:29.000 --> 05:32:34.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: So we're not expecting, we're expecting the same negative growth. We're not expecting...

asr-007494
05:32:34.000 --> 05:32:35.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Yes, for agriculture.

asr-007495
05:32:35.000 --> 05:32:40.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Okay. And where else did we experience negative growth other than agriculture?

asr-007496
05:32:40.000 --> 05:32:48.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: Of course, construction because of the slowdown in public spending last 2026.

asr-007497
05:32:48.000 --> 05:32:54.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Public or is it, is there a steeper negative in the private or public construction?

asr-007498
05:32:54.000 --> 05:32:55.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: I think private also.

asr-007499
05:32:55.000 --> 05:32:56.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Private.

asr-007500
05:32:56.000 --> 05:33:01.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: So that's a problem since private spending is also down.

asr-007501
05:33:01.000 --> 05:33:10.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: So I guess that's the time government now should take the lead in the, in public spending.

asr-007502
05:33:10.000 --> 05:33:19.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Yes. Based on my experience in government, it's very difficult to implement infrastructure projects in the latter half of the year because of the, precisely because of the climate.

asr-007503
05:33:19.000 --> 05:33:22.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Now, let's see, usually you want to finish construction the first half of the year.

asr-007504
05:33:22.000 --> 05:33:23.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: The second half.

asr-007505
05:33:23.000 --> 05:33:24.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: The second half.

asr-007506
05:33:24.000 --> 05:33:33.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: So based on that general, general observation, do we see construction rebounding the next, the next half of this year?

asr-007507
05:33:33.000 --> 05:33:39.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: Yes, that's what they expect, the economic managers expect a rebound by first quarter.

asr-007508
05:33:39.000 --> 05:33:52.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Despite the, despite the difficult, the physical, I mean, the challenges usually present, traditionally present in the second half of the year, can we, I mean, let's be honest, I mean, kung hindi kaya, hindi kaya.

asr-007509
05:33:53.000 --> 05:33:55.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Kaya maging masyadong optimistic kung hindi talaga kaya.

asr-007510
05:33:55.000 --> 05:34:01.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Gumawa tayo ng projections na talagang realistic.

asr-007511
05:34:01.000 --> 05:34:14.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: Well, because of the El Nino, they're confident that the construction industry will be able to rebound, especially the public spending.

asr-007512
05:34:14.000 --> 05:34:21.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: As I mentioned, as mentioned earlier, that they, they think that there will be a momentum in the third and fourth quarter.

asr-007513
05:34:21.000 --> 05:34:22.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: Yes.

asr-007514
05:34:22.000 --> 05:34:33.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: Because the projects were awarded second half already and the procurement was also done third and fourth quarter.

asr-007515
05:34:33.000 --> 05:34:43.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: So expect them, they expect the momentum to carry on and be supplemented by the, by the early procurement in the first quarter.

asr-007516
05:34:43.000 --> 05:34:52.000
Senator Joseph Victor "JV" G. Ejercito: That they will, they will, they are working on so that at least on during the first quarter, it will already be felt, Your Honor, Sir President.

asr-007517
05:34:52.000 --> 05:34:56.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: So based on our projections, fourth quarter, we're not expecting a rebound.

asr-007518
05:34:56.000 --> 05:35:00.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: We're expecting a rebound first quarter of next year.

asr-007519
05:35:00.000 --> 05:35:02.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: That's, that's a realistic.

asr-007520
05:35:02.000 --> 05:35:03.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Yes.

asr-007521
05:35:03.000 --> 05:35:05.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: Is that a realistic assumption?

asr-007522
05:35:05.000 --> 05:35:07.000
Senator Mark A. Villar: So we're looking at maybe 2%.

